---
/**
 * p51 · Aperture
 *
 * Plozz's mark has always been a television, and a television is furniture:
 * a case, a bezel, a stand, a thing that sits on a table. None of that is
 * the app. What the app gives you is an opening onto something playing
 * somewhere else, so this draws the opening and the surface it is cut into,
 * and stops.
 *
 * Depth is measured, not drawn. Every pixel is stepped by its distance from
 * the silhouette — a contour, so the steps turn the corners instead of
 * running past them — and each step moves exactly one place along a single
 * Plozz ramp. No step is skipped anywhere in the set, which is why the wall
 * reads as one surface falling away rather than as stacked plates.
 *
 * The side rails are cut six steps deep and the head and sill five, because
 * a frame in section is deeper across than it is down. That difference is
 * what keeps this from reading as a raised key. The mitres where the two
 * meet are a consequence of the measuring, not decoration on top of it.
 *
 * Then it stops. The centre is one flat field, 14 by 10, because the face
 * has to sit on something quiet: a screen with tone in it is a screen with
 * something already on it, and there is nothing playing here.
 *
 * Eight blues including the family keyline. The face is the supplied one,
 * untouched — md, compact smile, gap 2, 8 across and 8 down — centred on the
 * opening at (16, 16) with a clear pixel of field on every side.
 */
import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

<MarkFrame size={size} title="Plozz — Aperture">
